\ ==== trace ( xt patterns -- … ) ====

\ Runs a quotation printing, to stderr, one line per op before it executes: the op
\ as see-compiled renders it, a bar, and the data stack (top four, deepest first).
\ patterns is an array of regexes: the first selects ops by their op text, the
\ rest (if any) keep a selected line only when one of them matches the whole
\ line; [ ] prints every line. The traced code's own output goes to stdout.

\ -- literals and an arithmetic op
[: 3 4 + :] [ ] trace . cr

\ -- a colon call with a head: the frame ops show, and the fused load
: sq | x | x x * ;
[: 5 sq :] [ ] trace . cr

\ -- a loop: the fused compare-and-branch and the back branch
: count-to | n | 0 begin dup n < while 1+ repeat ;
[: 2 count-to :] [ ] trace . cr

\ -- a deep stack shows the top four with an ellipsis
[: 1 2 3 4 5 6 :] [ ] trace .s cr clear

\ -- tracing ends with the quotation; the next line is untraced
[: 1 :] [ ] trace drop
"untraced" . cr

\ -- the first pattern selects ops by op text
[: 5 sq 2 + :] [ "^sq" ] trace . cr
[: 5 sq 2 + :] [ "^exit" ] trace . cr

\ -- further patterns keep a selected op only when one matches the whole line
[: 5 sq 2 + :] [ "^exit" "\| 27" ] trace . cr
[: 5 sq 2 + :] [ "." "\| .*7" ] trace . cr

\ -- the first pattern sees the op text only, so a stack pattern there selects nothing
[: 5 sq 2 + :] [ "\| .*7" ] trace . cr

\ -- a body a combinator runs gets a line naming the callee, so a word called only
\ through map is selectable by name
[: [ 1 2 3 ] ' sq map :] [ "^sq" ] trace . cr
[: [ 1 2 ] 10 [: a k | a k * sq :] curry map :] [ "sq" ] trace . cr

\ -- error cases (grouped): a non-string pattern, an invalid regex
[: 1 :] [ 3 ] ' trace catch . . cr                       \ (element 0 is a float)
[: 1 :] [ "(" ] ' trace catch . . cr                     \ (missing closing parenthesis)

\ -- combinator bodies and try-catch are traced too, but an xt on the stack prints
\ by dictionary index, which moves with the embedded library, so those cases are
\ not pinned here
